{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Charles Dean O'Banion (July 8, 1892 – November 10, 1924) was an American mobster who was the main rival of Johnny Torrio and Al Capone during the brutal Chicago bootlegging wars of the 1920s.","title":"Dean O'Banion"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The newspapers of his day made him better known as Dion O'Banion, although he never went by that first name.","title":"Dean O'Banion"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He led the North Side Gang until 1924, when he was murdered, reportedly by Frankie Yale, John Scalise and Albert Anselmi.","title":"Dean O'Banion"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Vincent Drucci, also known as "The Schemer" (born Vincenzo D'Ambrosio; 1898 – April 4, 1927), was an Sicilian-American mobster during Chicago's Prohibition era who was a member of the North Side Gang, Al Capone's best known rivals.","title":"Vincent Drucci"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A friend of Dean O'Banion, Drucci succeeded him by becoming co-leader.","title":"Vincent Drucci"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is the only US organized crime boss to have been killed by a policeman.","title":"Vincent Drucci"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Angelo "Bloody Angelo" Genna (February 3, 1898 – May 26, 1925) was a Chicago bootlegger and organized crime leader during the Prohibition era.","title":"Angelo Genna"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The leader of his own Sicilian crime family, he was best known for his war with the North Side Gang leader, Charles Dean O'Banion.","title":"Angelo Genna"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Genna masterminded the assassination of O'Banion in November 1924.","title":"Angelo Genna"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""I Seen a Man Die", also known as "I Never Seen a Man Cry", is the second single released from Scarface's third album, "The Diary".","title":"I Seen a Man Die"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Produced by N.O. Joe, Mike Dean and Scarface himself, "I Seen a Man Die" became a top 40 hit on the "Billboard" Hot 100, the first of two that Scarface had in his career.","title":"I Seen a Man Die"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It peaked at 37 on the Billboard Hot 100.","title":"I Seen a Man Die"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The song is a tale of a young male released from prison after seven years looking for a better life only to get caught up on the crime side again and robbed by his enemies only to die in the hospital while feeling regrets.","title":"I Seen a Man Die"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The song also has a music video released which mirrors Scarface's lyrics.","title":"I Seen a Man Die"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Scarface (also known as Scarface: The Shame of the Nation and The Shame of a Nation) is a 1932 American pre-Code gangster film starring Paul Muni as Antonio "Tony" Camonte.","title":"Scarface (1932 film)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was produced by Howard Hughes and Howard Hawks and directed by Hawks.","title":"Scarface (1932 film)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The story is based on Armitage Trail's 1929 novel of the same name, which is loosely based on the rise and fall of Al Capone.","title":"Scarface (1932 film)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The film features Ann Dvorak as Camonte's sister, and also stars Karen Morley, Osgood Perkins, and Boris Karloff.","title":"Scarface (1932 film)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The plot centers on gang warfare and police intervention when rival gangs fight over control of Chicago.","title":"Scarface (1932 film)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" A version of the Saint Valentine's Day Massacre is also depicted.","title":"Scarface (1932 film)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Cuban Marimba Band (previously known as La Paloma) was an influential Tanzanian big band from the city of Morogoro.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was founded in 1948 by Salim Abdullah, who had previously created another band called Morogoro Jazz Band (which, somewhat ironically, later became Cuban Marimba's main rival).","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":"Salum Abdallah was a prolific songwriter and musician.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He was a "chotara" i.e. a person of mixed races.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" His band's name speaks volumes on the influence of Cuban music in the 40s and 50s in East Africa.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" He played guitar and mandolin.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The main themes of his music were, the joy and pain of love, life wisdom, the good and evil of man kind and even politics.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" For about twenty years, until Abdullah's sudden death in a car accident in 1965, Cuban Marimba was one of the most popular muziki wa dansi (dance music) bands in Tanzania.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" One of his popular tunes is Mkono wa Idd, which is usually played in the radio during Idd festivities.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Just before his death, he released a twist tune, a departure from his suave rumba style," Hapo zamani sana" about the horrible colonial days in South Africa.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Then in another, he paid tribute to Tanzanian women, "Wanawake wa Tanzania wazuri sana."","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" Abdullah was replaced by Juma Kilaza, but the band declined.","title":"Cuban Marimba Band"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Al Capone
