{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Bologna Process is a series of ministerial meetings and agreements between European countries to ensure comparability in the standards and quality of higher-education qualifications.","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The process has created the European Higher Education Area under the Lisbon Recognition Convention.","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is named after the University of Bologna, where the Bologna declaration was signed by education ministers from 29 European countries in 1999.","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The process was opened to other countries in the European Cultural Convention of the Council of Europe, and governmental meetings have been held in Prague (2001), Berlin (2003), Bergen (2005), London (2007) and Leuven (2009).","title":"Bologna Process"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The European Master's in Translation (EMT) is a partnership project between the "Directorate-General for Translation" (DGT) of the "European Commission" and a number of universities from a wide range of European countries.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" EMT is a quality label for translation programmes that offer a Master's degree.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The DGT awards the label to higher education programmes that meet the EMT quality standards for translator training.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Master programmes that meet these standards can become members of the EMT network.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The initial project was launched in 2006 and the first network was set up in December 2009, following a rigorous selection in which applicant university programmes were evaluated by academic assessors.","title":"European Master's in Translation"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" This first EMT Network consis